[36m(main_task pid=2827894)[0m qid: D14365479 abstract: The performance of search and recommendation tasks relies on the quality of the semantic representation learned for user and item queries as well as the query representation (ID). While the design of the user and item queries and ID representations has attracted significant interest, there is a lack of study regarding the representation learned for text queries. It is challenging to simultaneously model the similarity of the text query with the input of other queries, as well as the latent factors of the queries learned in an unsupervised manner. In this paper, we model the text query using convolutional neural network (CNN) for mapping the text sequence to a vector space, which is further combined with the historical interaction sequence of the query user to approximate the target interaction. Based on the interacted queries with text inputs in the sequences, we extract their latent factors as a document tensor. The interactive behaviors for different text queries of the same target query can be extracted for reflecting the semantics and relevance between the two queries. Finally, the latent factors of the text queries and semantic query vector are combined with the vector representing the target query, the vector encoding the properties of the query and item, and the historical interaction sequences to form the final representation of the query item pair, respectively. The experimental results on the query log dataset from a commercial service show the effectiveness of the proposed model compared with the existing works.
