[36m(main_task pid=2827894)[0m <information><html><body><p><strong>King Midas: Grapes and the Love of Gold</strong></p><p>In Greek mythology, King Midas was a legendary king who was granted a wish by Dionysus (the god of wine and revelry), but ended up in despair when he realized that everything he touched turned to gold, even his food, drink, and his beloved daughter. To his plea for redemption, Dionysus took pity and granted him another wish.</p><p>Dionysus instructed King Midas to rinse his hands in the waters of the Pactolus River (in modern-day Turkey), where all his evils would be washed away, and his touch would no longer have the ability to transmute objects into gold.</p><p>After washing in the Pactolus River, Midas's hands returned to their original state. However, when he rubbed the ground at the river's edge, it did transform into gold, a minor reminder of the past folly.</p><p>This fabled event is thought to be the source of the gold deposits in the river, and the story symbolizes the theme of moderation, where extreme desires lead to misfortune.</p><p>King Midas was transformed into a dolphin for attempting to abduct Ariadne, a daughter of King Minos of Crete. Dionysus was incensed by this action, so he transformed Midas into a dolphin. According to another myth, Midas was also said to be transformed into a donkey, but the dolphin transformation is more commonly known.</p></body></html></information>
