[36m(main_task pid=2827894)[0m As time went on, "Can't Touch This" would become hip-hop's first crossover hit in addition to becoming a number-one hit in most other Western markets, especially with Eurocentric countries. It became a fixture in world music festivals such as Madonna's 1991 Who's That Girl Tour, or even being covered by David Koepsell (She's a Globo Girl). After a relatively weak singles chart performance, a second Warner Music tour on "Unplugged" proved an international hit, including Belgium, the Netherlands, UK, Ireland, Norway, Japan, and Sweden. It led to the 1991 follow-up hit, "Pray," which peaked at No. 2 in the U.S., the first post-Motherland Top 10 for Jackson. This was followed up by its 1994 Fox broadcast, "Runnin'," and its companion film featuring the Red Hot Chili Peppers (who contributed to "Life Can Be Your Drug," "Diss" or "Face Straight" sung from Jazzy Britney Spears). (18000174)
[36m(main_task pid=2827894)[0m The song became a international smash hit, peaking at No. 3 in the U.S., No. 1 in Canada, and the U.K., the first Euro-soul hit single for Warner Bros. Records since "Will You Go Choo Choo" by Syl Johnson in 1966 and Grammy Award-winning "Bugle Call Rag" by Bing Crosby. (18000169)
[36m(main_task pid=2827894)[0m The people who created the original Britney Spears TV film titled "Britney: Sex and the City" were pleased with the quality of the television movie, but it was never aired. This was due to Jackson's request that all songs be released under the Fox category, something that remained a mystery until Hip-Hop started gaining popularity. In fact, "Can't Touch This" earned many of the 1991 Britney Spears calendar covers. (18000200)
