[36m(main_task pid=2827894)[0m  apsida, Infraclass Archosauromorpha, Superorder Archosauria, Order Dinosauria, Suborder Theopoda. Class reptile Originally, dinosaurs were classified by paleontologists based on superficial characteristics, leading to a variety of orders, such as Saurischia, Ornithischia, Saurofootia, Pyroscopiformes, and Ornithoscelida. An early insight into dinosaur relationships came in the 1870s, when evolutionist Charles Darwin noted a developmental peculiarity that puzzled him. Darwin knew that when cattle are born they have two toes, but cows also have an extra set of "domed knobs on the legs" which do not develop into toes. These are traces of the cow's ancestor having had four toes; their descendants have lost the extra pair. Darwin realised that humans are the same way, having evolved from ancestors with longer limbs, tails, and a heavier skull. He concluded that all living and extinct forms of organisms could be understood in terms of gradual evolutionary change, governed by the natural selection of small variations that give an advantage in survival and reproduction. The evolutionary relationships and "family tree" of the dinosaurs (family cladogram) have been the subject of debate for over a century, and are far from being settled. Traditionally, dinosaurs were classified according to the classic Linnaean rank-based system. More recent methods use phylogenetic bracketing, in which organisms are grouped based on their evolutionary history. Many of the traditional groups of dinosaurs are no longer considered taxonomically valid according to modern cladistic principles.
