[36m(main_task pid=2827894)[0m   June solstice occurs around June 21 each year, when Earth’s North Pole is tilted 23.44 degrees toward the Sun. It’s called a solstice because Earth has a stationary point in its orbit, which acts as a stator for the spinning planet. From the Southern Hemisphere, the June solstice is called the winter solstice because it marks the longest night and shortest day of the year in this part of the world. It’s the reverse for the North Pole, which observes a yearly minimum of solar input (relative to the rest of the year) during the summer solstice. Do not confuse the June solstice (which comes just after summer in the Northern Hemisphere) with the June equinox. The Northern Hemisphere’s spring equinox comes around March 20 each year, while the Southern Hemisphere’s autumn equinox also happens around March 20 each year. The equinoxes come just after the start of spring (when the length of day and night are equal) and halfway between the June solstice and the December solstice.
